  "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,4),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}-(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+6x+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(-{x}^{3}+6x+y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}+6(1)+(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(1,4).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
